      "body": {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "tone": "definition",
            "title": "Petrochemicals",
            "text": "Petrochemicals are compounds obtained mainly from crude oil and natural gas by refining and transformation."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Petrochemicals are used to make many everyday items in packaging, construction, textiles, electronics and medicine. Synthetic chemicals made from petrochemicals are their derivatives, and these are very important industrial products. Petrochemicals are changed into their derivatives by chemical reactions such as polymerisation, oxidation or substitution."
          },
          {
            "type": "table",
            "caption": "Table 31.5 Derivatives of petrochemicals and their uses",
            "header": [
              "Petrochemical",
              "Derived chemical or product",
              "Uses"
            ],
            "rows": [
              [
                "Ethene",
                "Poly(ethene) (plastic)",
                "Bags, bottles, packaging"
              ],
              [
                "Propene",
                "Poly(propene)",
                "Containers, automotive parts"
              ],
              [
                "Benzene",
                "Styrene, then polystyrene (poly(phenylethene))",
                "Disposable cups, insulation"
              ],
              [
                "Toluene",
                "TNT (trinitrotoluene)",
                "Explosives"
              ],
              [
                "Xylene",
                "Polyester fibres",
                "Textiles, clothing (fleece, jerseys) and upholstery"
              ],
              [
                "Butadiene",
                "Synthetic rubber (e.g. styrene butadiene rubber)",
                "Tyres, footwear"
              ]
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
